WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.020
Every day, in the DNA of your cells, some cytosines lose their amino group and turn

2
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:14.420
into uracil. Uracil pairs with adenine, so the next replication puts an adenine

3
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:19.680
opposite it. The round after that leaves an A·T pair where a G·C pair had been.

4
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.940
That is a mutation made by chemistry alone.

5
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.560
So how does a cell find a few wrong bases among billions of right ones?

6
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.490
The answer lies in the double helix, which holds its information twice.

7
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.890
If one strand is damaged, the cell cuts out the bad stretch and copies the partner

8
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:43.830
strand. In base excision repair, a glycosylase flips the uracil out and cuts it from

9
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:49.170
its sugar. Another enzyme nicks the backbone there, and a polymerase inserts the

10
00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.890
correct nucleotide, using the partner strand.

11
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.010
A ligase seals the nick.

12
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.370
Ultraviolet light does bulkier damage.

13
00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:02.990
It joins two neighboring pyrimidines on one strand into a dimer that distorts the

14
00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:08.770
helix. Nucleotide excision repair senses the distortion rather than one chemical

15
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.790
change. It cuts the damaged strand on both sides and removes a short piece.

16
00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:18.690
A polymerase fills the gap from the partner strand, and a ligase seals it.

17
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.660
A mismatch is two normal bases paired wrongly, such as G with T. Neither is damaged,

18
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.560
so the cell must find the new strand, because the template was correct.

19
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:36.740
In E. coli, the template carries methyl groups at GATC sites, and the new strand is

20
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.100
briefly unmethylated.

21
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.880
MutS binds the mismatch, MutL joins it, and MutH nicks the unmethylated strand.

22
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.460
That strand is removed past the mismatch and rebuilt.

23
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:52.903
A break through both strands removes the copying option at one spot.

24
00:01:53.803 --> 00:01:54.863
Picture a rope cut through.

25
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:59.643
You can knot the ends, or splice in a matching length from an identical rope.

26
00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:05.883
Non-homologous end joining knots the ends: a ring-shaped protein, Ku, grips each end

27
00:02:06.063 --> 00:02:07.163
and a ligase joins them.

28
00:02:07.923 --> 00:02:12.803
It uses no template, so if the ends needed trimming, a few bases may be gained or

29
00:02:12.803 --> 00:02:13.803
lost.

30
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:17.677
Homologous recombination splices from a matching rope.

31
00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:24.657
Nucleases trim the ends to single-stranded tails, and Rad51 coats them and invades a

32
00:02:24.657 --> 00:02:30.417
matching duplex. The invading end primes synthesis, copying the information lost at

33
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:35.577
the break. The best template is the sister chromatid, the identical copy made in

34
00:02:35.577 --> 00:02:41.017
replication. One rule runs through almost all repair: the information is held twice.
